Some of these are probably answered in excrutiating detail in a spoiler
file, but I'll throw them out in the interests of picking the brains of more
experienced tacticians and strategists ;)

My usual course lately has been thusly: Descend to Sokoban, clear it fully
if possible - if the zoo up top is too scary, back off, retreat to Gnomish
Mines. My logic here is that the mines frequently have large numbers of
critters with wands, open spaces, and lots of traps, whereas the descent to
Sokoban is generally fairly mild, I acquire useful items along the way, and
Sokoban itself is a great source of food, rings, wands, some gold, and a
handy item - even if doing it every game is horrendously boring :( I wish it
was a more interesting set of tactical challenges, rather than a static
puzzle game.

Clear out the Gnomish Mines, acquire the luckstone. If the altar is
co-aligned, do some saccing to acquire an early gift or two. If the altar is
NOT co-aligned, do a careful evaluation of my inventory. If I can create a
fortress from a scroll of earth, preferably have reflection from Sokoban,
lots of hp and good ac, strong weapon, quick escape methods, and a happy
diety, I may attempt a conversion. Otherwise I simply bypass it - usually
another altar to be found simply in the main dungeon (besides, handy to have
priest around for protection).

Descend to quest level, marking any levels with vaults on them, hoping to
discover Ludios. Mark Ludios and continue on. Once at the quest level,
evaluate equipment, possibly tackle it now. If uncertain, either proceed
part way to gauge difficulty, or proceed on to Medusa and Castle. During
this descent, dig out walls between all up and down stairs. Bring along a
stash of useful items, store it on the lowest level with an altar in a
closed one slot room with elbereth engraved. Move this stash down as I go
down.

I find the quest to be quite entertaining (particularly since I'm just now
reaching it regularly with new characters), Medusa boring, and Ludios/Castle
to be fun. Sokoban is by far the most boring :(

Once this stage of the game is done with, I begin preparations for the end
game. Ideally, I'll have already acquired full intrinsics (possibly lacking
disintegration), -10 protection, maxed attributes, some form of dragon scale
mail, reflection, magic resistance, holy water (I'm now adding unholy water
to this list, there are some very useful cursed items, which makes precious
little sense to me...), a STRONG weapon, a strong backup weapon, ranged
weapons (I'll up this priority - I've go to say though, bow/arrow,
xbow/quarrels, sling/stones seem almost useless in Nethack. They're just
such a pain to use, even a stack of Daggers is more useful and comfortable
to use, and even Shuriken and Darts are pointless due to breakage), useful
wands, useful rings and backup rings, useful scrolls and backup scrolls,
useful tools, means of mapping (any good method other than spell of magic
map? scrolls seem difficult to acquire), lighting (are there any good means
besides a magic lamp? lamps + oil seems kind of heavy), healing and quick
escape (teleportation, digging, scare monster). I consider a pet optional,
but if I have a magic whistle, and some means of taming or polymorphing, I
may attempt to bring along a friend. I find dealing with them to be
something of a hassle in general, as I don't always find a magic whistle.

I'm not yet certain what is a 'good' number of hp/pp for the endgame. I'm
also not clear on all the methods of raising each permanently, nor do I know
how to gain levels past ~16 easily. I just recently tried nurse dancing
successfully for max hp, but scrolls of genocide aren't common. I've used
blessed potions of full healing as well. For raising levels, I know of
wraiths, but as with nurses, the scrolls are a problem. I know gain energy
raises max pp, anything else?

Related to this prep, I hopefully acquire some water walking boots, to make
scroll/potion blanking easier - is there another method for doing this that
I'm missing? Also, is there a safe/safer way to acquire disintegration
resistance? Black Dragons make me *very* nervous, since Reflection isn't
guaranteed.

Similarly, how on earth do you get more magic markers? I tried polypiling a
ton of tools with no luck, and I rarely find any in a given game. I
generally wind up burning wishes on them, which is usually just enough to
fully *proof and enchant my armor and weapons, maybe genocide a species or
two, and that's about it. More would be very nice.

Are there any means of charging other than scrolls of charging? Any good
means of acquiring same?

Once this preparation is complete, I head down into Gehennom (or recently,
Heck^2 with the patch :)). I absolutely detest this part of the game. I did
just discover that magic mapping makes this _far_ less annoying, and autodig
also helps eliminate a ton of the pain. Without magic mapping though... ugh.
Not fun. Boring, tedious, drawn out. Even more horrible that the Amulet
draws this part of the game out even more. The special levels are the only
interesting parts of this run. Find up and down stairs, dig a path between
each. Locate alternate routes, procure opening artifacts, find vibrating
square, dump artifacts here.

Perform a check of my kit again at this point, make sure I'm ready for this
step. Whack the Wizard, get the Bell, trigger the invocation. Descend into
sanctuary, acquire the Amulet. Now begin my mad dash for the top :) I asked
some questions in my YASD thread about the Wiz, so without rehashing, I'll
just say that I'm not yet fully comfortable/prepared/knowledgable about the
ascent. My first Ascension earlier this month was pretty painless. He showed
up, I whacked him, moved on. He didn't show up much (though the Amulet did
drag me down a few times). My second near Ascension, not so much - I wound
up mired at the bottom of the dungeon in an hours long battle that ended in
my ultimate defeat.

That's the general structure of my play currently, more or less. I'm finding
I'm having longer character life expectancies, and exploring new avenues in
the game, though I suspect/expect there is plenty more to explore and learn.

